      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we need to analyze the form of the hyperbola and identify its key parameters.\n\nThe standard form of a hyperbola that opens up and down (where the \\(y^2\\) term is positive) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nFor the given equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we can identify:\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11\n\\]\n\nThe vertices of a hyperbola in this form are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Therefore, the vertices are at:\n\\[\n(0, \\pm \\sqrt{27})\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the distance between these vertices. The distance between \\((0, \\sqrt{27})\\) and \\((0, -\\sqrt{27})\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{27} - (-\\sqrt{27}))^2} = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{27} + \\sqrt{27})^2} = \\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{27})^2} = \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 27} = \\sqrt{108} = \\sqrt{36 \\cdot 3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nWhat is the sentiment of the following movie (choose your answer from the options) review sentence?\ndraconian \nOPT: (a). negative. (b). positive.\nThe answer is:\n\n(a).",
