      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we first need to identify the standard form of the hyperbola and its parameters.\n\nThe given equation is in the standard form of a hyperbola that opens vertically:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1.\n\\]\nBy comparing this with the given equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we can see that:\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11.\n\\]\nThe vertices of the hyperbola are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). From the equation, we have \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nThe distance between the vertices is therefore:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
